# NOTICE

`dsh-delete-guard-plus` is a merge of two bodies of work, plus new material.

## 1. `dsh-delete-guard` — upstream design that this plugin merges

- Author: deepestevan
- Package: `dsh-delete-guard` (https://www.npmjs.com/package/dsh-delete-guard)
- Repository: https://github.com/deepestevan/dsh-delete-guard
- License: MIT

What this plugin took from it:

- the idea of mounting on `tools/pre-execute` and answering `deny` rather than
  `ask`, with a redirect instead of an approval prompt;
- a registered `file_trash` tool as the only permitted deletion path, plus
  `trash_status`;
- the Recycle Bin bridge implemented with `Microsoft.VisualBasic.FileIO` /
  `SHFileOperationW` semantics, and the refusal of UNC and FAT/exFAT volumes
  where a "recycle" is really an erase;
- the operand guard for direct `file_trash` calls (drive root, Windows
  directory, Program Files, Recycle Bin storage, session workspace root);
- a system-prompt policy section so the model knows the rule before it writes a
  command that cannot run;
- the refusal of `Clear-RecycleBin`, `shred`/`sdelete`/`srm`/`cipher /w`,
  `[System.IO.File]::Delete`, `robocopy /MIR`, `-EncodedCommand` and
  `Invoke-Expression`.

Its code was not copied verbatim: the modules were rewritten so that the rule
engine is shared with the analyzer below, and several defects were fixed on the
way (see `CHANGELOG.md`, entries `2.0.0`).

## 2. Quoting-typo analyzer — written for one incident

A single command destroyed most of a user's C: drive:

```powershell
$p='C:\Windows\Temp\x-chrome'; cmd /c "rmdir /s /q \"$p\""
```

PowerShell escapes with a backtick. `\"` therefore closed the string, and cmd
received `rmdir /s /q \` — a recursive delete of the volume root. The upstream
guard reasons about delete *verbs* and delete *targets*; this failure is about
*quoting*, so a second analyzer was written and then merged in:

- PowerShell strings are tokenized with PowerShell escape rules, and `\"` inside
  one is treated as a live grenade whenever a delete verb is present;
- cmd text is tokenized without backslash escaping (cmd escapes with `^`), which
  is what makes `"\"` read as the root path it actually is;
- nested interpreters (`cmd /c …`, `powershell -Command …`, `bash -c …`) are
  re-analyzed under their own dialect;
- targets that are variables, subexpressions, globs or empty are refused as
  unprovable, because a delete whose operand only exists at run time cannot be
  reviewed by anyone;
- path depth and protected roots (Windows, Program Files, user profiles, `.dsh`,
  `.ssh`, shadow copies, registry, certificate stores, event logs) are refused.

## 3. The ten-round corpus work

The rule set was exercised against ten independent 100,000-line corpora of
Windows-shaped commands (one domain per round: filesystem, services, network,
registry, storage, identity, toolchains, logs, devices, text/encoding).
Findings and the rule changes they produced are recorded in
`docs/rounds/round-NN-review.md`.

## 4. Intended use / 使用前提

A personal tool: written by one DSH for one user, after one real incident. Not a product, no
maintenance commitment, and no claim that it catches every accident. It decides from command-line
**text**, so a delete performed inside a binary, a path assembled at run time, a non-Windows
platform, or a race is outside what it can see.

Adoption belongs to the person whose machine it runs on:

- the DSH states what the plugin does and what it is permitted to do — it runs in-process, reads
  the filesystem, can spawn `powershell.exe`, registers three tools, and takes over the
  pre-dispatch decision of every tool call;
- the user agrees before it is installed;
- the user has their own DSH read `lib/*.js` and `scripts/recycle-bin.ps1` and report, rule by rule,
  what is blocked, what is allowed, and where it is not confident;
- that DSH then adapts the rules to the user's situation — drop families that do not apply, add
  their own protected paths, decide whether literal safe deletes may run;
- and removal is one line: deleting `dsh-delete-guard-plus` from `dsh.profile.bundles` restores the
  previous behaviour, because the plugin installs no service, task or registry entry.

The full handover checklist, in both languages, is in the READMEs.

